Boudewijn Waijers wrote:

> Well, actually you should even identify it before you enchant it the
> *first* time. After all, you might just have found that rare blessed +5
> long sword that was lying around waiting to be found!

I'd like to point out here what I think we all know, that is that 
mathematical certainty is not a prerequisite for action.  It's not 
necessary (or even wise) to keep searching prior to every movement until 
I'm *certain* there are no traps i'm about to step in.  It turns out the 
chances will never be zero, but they may eventually be small enough for me 
to live with the risk.  In fact I might be willing to live with the risk 
(most of the time) without searching at all.

Yes there's a penalty for enchanting the weapon the first time and being 
wrong and having it evaporate.  But there is also a penalty for 
waiting 'til after identification before enchanting it: you might be killed 
in the meantime and +1 might have saved your skin.

Each person will have to decide for themselves which risk is the more 
dangerous.  There is no risk-free action one can take.

-Jamie


